Containers landed 2026-08-28 (commit 3d92fbc) as the vertical slice. The
mechanism is settled; the other six are the same shape, one at a time.

The pattern, in three parts:

  1. API::Docker::Role::Entity  -- already there. Provides `client`
     (ro, weak_ref) and the _entity_attributes hook that
     API::Docker::Role::Type's BUILDARGS reads.
  2. API::Docker::Role::Entity::<Resource> -- `with
     'API::Docker::Role::Entity'; requires 'id';` (or 'name' for volumes),
     the convenience methods, and at the bottom of the same file the
     apply_roles_to_package loop over the generated classes it belongs on,
     with the clash check against ->docker_attributes.
  3. API::Docker::API::<Resource> -- _wrap/_wrap_list take the class to
     build, since list and inspect are two definitions.

Per resource, the two classes to compose onto are the list-entry definition
and the inspect-response definition; check the swagger rather than assuming
they exist -- volumes answer both with the same `Volume` definition, which
makes it one class, not two.

What each conversion has to carry over, as containers did:

  - the entity's POD, minus what the generated =attr blocks now say. What
    does NOT come from the spec is the list-vs-inspect comparison; for
    containers it became "The two container shapes" in
    API::Docker::API::Containers.
  - every internal caller of the old CamelCase accessors. Containers had one
    that no grep of the entity class would have found:
    _assert_container_running read ->State on the inspect result inside
    API/Containers.pm itself.
  - the tests, whose accessors go snake_case. Say per test whether the claim
    survived the rename or was replaced.

Note the return shape changes publicly each time (->Id becomes ->id). The
containers slice raised that for the maintainer; the same answer applies to
the rest.

Blocked on nothing, but read k83 first -- if the model stops croaking on a
type mismatch, one workaround in Containers.pm comes back out.

